<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <parent>
        <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-clients</artifactId>
        <groupId>bz.stub.parallelconsumer</groupId>
        <version>0.6.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    </parent>
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <name>Parallel Consumer - Proxy Client C++</name>
    <artifactId>parallel-consumer-proxy-client-cpp</artifactId>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <!--
        Thin Maven wrapper over bin/build-client.sh, which builds this module IN A CONTAINER: C++ is
        one of the two languages mise cannot serve here, because gRPC and protobuf arrive as system
        dev packages rather than as a versioned toolchain (astubbs#242,
        docs/inflight/parked-containerised-toolchains-and-runtime.md). The commands below run ONLY
        under -Dpc.foreignClients - see the foreign-clients profile in the clients aggregator for why
        opt-in is load-bearing - so an ordinary scoped build of this module starts no container at
        all.

        It calls the script rather than `cmake` directly because a host cmake would need
        libgrpc++-dev, libprotobuf-dev and protobuf-compiler-grpc installed, which is the problem the
        image solves. The script owns which route a language takes; this pom only names the language.
        The test-phase invocation runs the EXTRACTED artifact on the host, which is what asserts the
        static link - see the script's header. (Its flag cannot be written in an XML comment: two
        consecutive hyphens end one.)

        The path is relative to this module rather than ${maven.multiModuleProjectDirectory}: that
        property is set by the mvn launcher and is empty when the build is driven by an IDE or an
        embedder, which would silently invoke "/bin/build-client.sh".

        THIS MODULE CONFIGURES NO maven-clean-plugin FILESET, AND THAT IS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN AN
        OMISSION. Every other foreign client declares one, because its native toolchain writes
        somewhere Maven has never heard of; the container route writes exactly ONE host-side path,
        the export directory bin/build-client.sh computes as <module>/target/container (grep
        `out_dir` in that script). That is inside ${project.build.directory}, so the default clean
        already deletes the extracted binaries, the dynamic control and link-report.txt - by the
        same mechanism that keeps the promise for a Java module, with nothing added. Measured on
        this module: build, `target/container/pc-cpp-selftest` present, `mvn clean`, gone, rebuild
        green.

        WHAT MUST NOT BE ADDED HERE. Everything else this build produces is SHARED: the
        debian:trixie base image, the BuildKit layer cache, and the apt cache mounts. Several agents
        and worktrees run against this machine at once, so deleting any of them would throw away
        output nobody asked this module to rebuild, and would turn a colleague's 1-second cached
        build into a minutes-long one. bin/build-client.sh's `build_natively` records the same
        reasoning for the native route. They are this language's ~/.m2, and `mvn clean` does not
        empty ~/.m2.

        And nothing here may list `target/container` explicitly either: that would be a second copy
        of the script's `out_dir`, free to drift from it, deleting something already deleted.

        Skeleton until the C++ wave of the language-proxy plan starts; that wave owns everything in
        this directory except the build environment (Dockerfile, toolchain-smoke/), and may replace
        the smoke target once it has one of its own.
    -->
    <properties>
        <pc.foreign.skip>false</pc.foreign.skip>
        <pc.foreign.build.executable>${project.basedir}/../../bin/build-client.sh</pc.foreign.build.executable>
        <pc.foreign.build.args>cpp</pc.foreign.build.args>
        <pc.foreign.test.executable>${project.basedir}/../../bin/build-client.sh</pc.foreign.test.executable>
        <pc.foreign.test.args>cpp --test</pc.foreign.test.args>
    </properties>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
